    "case_id": "466",
    "case_summary": "A patient in their early 60s presented with a 2-week history of chest pain during exertion. The chest pain consistently presented during physical activity. The patient was a nonsmoker with no family history of premature coronary artery disease.\n\nThe baseline 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) showed no substantial abnormalities.\n\n- Stress ECG (Treadmill Test):    - Patient developed left bundle-branch block (LBBB) in stage 2 at heart rate of 107 beats per minute with chest pain    - LBBB resolved following exercise cessation with immediate angina relief    - No ischemic or memory T waves in pre- and post-exercise ECGs        Image Title: ECG Findings of Treadmill Test    Image Description A: Stage 2 treadmill test showing normal sinus rhythm and left bundle-branch block at heart rate of 107 beats per minute    Image Description B: Recovery phase showing normal sinus rhythm and spontaneous disappearance of LBBB        - Coronary computed tomography angiography showed no abnormalities",
